{
 "title": "Why We Have Seasons",
 "lang": "en",
 "steps": [
  {
   "title": "The question",
   "say": "Seasons come from Earth's tilt, not from how far we are from the Sun. Let's see how.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "id": "t1",
     "op": "write",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 50,
     "text": "Why We Have Seasons",
     "size": 56,
     "anchor": "middle",
     "underline": true
    },
    {
     "id": "q1",
     "op": "write",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 150,
     "text": "Is it because Earth gets closer to the Sun?",
     "size": 40,
     "anchor": "middle",
     "color": "gray"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Sun and orbit",
   "say": "Earth travels around the Sun once a year along a nearly circular path.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "id": "sun",
     "op": "ellipse",
     "cx": 800,
     "cy": 520,
     "rx": 60,
     "ry": 60,
     "color": "red",
     "fill": "red"
    },
    {
     "id": "sunl",
     "op": "write",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 600,
     "text": "Sun",
     "size": 32,
     "anchor": "middle"
    },
    {
     "id": "orb",
     "op": "ellipse",
     "cx": 800,
     "cy": 520,
     "rx": 500,
     "ry": 210,
     "color": "gray",
     "width": 3,
     "dash": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Earth with a tilt",
   "say": "Earth's axis is tilted about 23.5 degrees, and it always points toward the same direction in space.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "id": "e1",
     "op": "ellipse",
     "cx": 300,
     "cy": 520,
     "rx": 50,
     "ry": 50,
     "color": "blue",
     "fill": "blue"
    },
    {
     "id": "ax1",
     "op": "line",
     "x1": 272,
     "y1": 600,
     "x2": 328,
     "y2": 440,
     "color": "ink",
     "width": 4
    },
    {
     "id": "e1l",
     "op": "write",
     "x": 300,
     "y": 620,
     "text": "tilt 23.5°",
     "size": 30,
     "anchor": "middle"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Same tilt, other side",
   "say": "Half a year later Earth is on the opposite side, but the axis still leans the same way.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "id": "e2",
     "op": "ellipse",
     "cx": 1300,
     "cy": 520,
     "rx": 50,
     "ry": 50,
     "color": "blue",
     "fill": "blue"
    },
    {
     "id": "ax2",
     "op": "line",
     "x1": 1272,
     "y1": 600,
     "x2": 1328,
     "y2": 440,
     "color": "ink",
     "width": 4
    },
    {
     "id": "e2l",
     "op": "write",
     "x": 1300,
     "y": 620,
     "text": "same tilt",
     "size": 30,
     "anchor": "middle"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Summer in the north",
   "say": "On the right the northern half leans toward the Sun, so sunlight is more direct and days are longer: summer.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "id": "r1",
     "op": "line",
     "x1": 1130,
     "y1": 520,
     "x2": 1235,
     "y2": 490,
     "color": "red",
     "arrow": true
    },
    {
     "id": "s1",
     "op": "write",
     "x": 1300,
     "y": 330,
     "text": "North: summer",
     "size": 38,
     "anchor": "middle",
     "color": "red"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Winter in the north",
   "say": "On the left the northern half leans away, so light is slanted and spread out, days are shorter: winter.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "id": "r2",
     "op": "line",
     "x1": 470,
     "y1": 520,
     "x2": 365,
     "y2": 520,
     "color": "red",
     "arrow": true,
     "dash": true
    },
    {
     "id": "s2",
     "op": "write",
     "x": 300,
     "y": 330,
     "text": "North: winter",
     "size": 38,
     "anchor": "middle",
     "color": "blue"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Takeaway",
   "say": "So seasons are caused by the tilt of Earth's axis, which changes how directly sunlight hits us through the year.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "id": "k1",
     "op": "write",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 790,
     "text": "Tilt → sunlight angle + day length → seasons",
     "size": 40,
     "anchor": "middle",
     "color": "green"
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}